  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nAnother hot day is ahead for us throughout the Mid-South as a\nstrong, 594+ dm ridge remains centered over the southern\nMississippi River Valley. This morning's surface observations\nalready show temperatures climbing into the low 90s, which will\npeak around 100 through the majority of the region. Tropical\ndewpoints (> 70 F) will therefore help yield afternoon heat index\nvalues above 110 F across the entire region. As such, an Extreme\nHeat Warning is in effect area wide. A similar forecast is\nexpected tomorrow where 110 F heat indices are forecast with the\nExtreme Heat Warning still in effect as dangerous heat continues\nthrough the southern and central CONUS. CAMs show that some\nshowers could develop in the afternoon and evening across\nsouthern MO and northern AR, potentially tracking into portions\nof eastern AR and the MO Bootheel. However, the mixing required\nto reach the convective temperature (105+ F) would bring LCLs\nabove 2000 m, promoting enough evaporation of precipitation to\nprevent meaningful precipitation from making it to the surface\nand reducing heat relief.\n\nThe ridge will continue to sit over the region into early next\nweek with daily highs in the middle to upper 90s, potentially\nclimbing over 100 F in some areas Monday and Tuesday. One\npossible caveat to the forecast next week is that the dewpoints\nwill decrease through daily mixing of mid-level, dry air to the\nsurface over the next several days. Lower dewpoints would mean\nlower heat index values, which are currently forecast to fall\nbelow 110 F Monday and Tuesday with some areas falling below\n105 F Tuesday. However, afternoon temperatures will remain\ndangerous and will warrant new heat headlines for some areas, but\nhave deferred this decision to a later forecast issuance to get a\nbetter gauge on spatial trends of where heat headlines will be\nneeded.\n\nBy the middle of next week and into the following weekend,\nensembles are slowly converging on a new pattern taking hold over\nthe eastern CONUS. Through the early parts of this upcoming week,\nan area of troughing should begin to amplify just off the west\ncoast  of OR/WA in the Pacific. Downstream ridging will amplify\nin response across the Rockies, subjecting the westerlies across\nthe northern Plains and Midwest to a northwesterly deflection.\nThe end result is an area of troughing gradually forming over the\neastern CONUS that would act to cool the region. But the level of\ncooling is still relative with highs beyond Wednesday forecast to\nremain in the 90s with afternoon heat index values approaching\nthe triple digits. Some rain is possible as troughing settles in\ntowards the end of the week, but any precipitation would be\nassociated with diurnal heating and is currently expected to be\nisolated to scattered in nature. Regardless, for now, some relief\nis finally beginning to appear in the forecast that would bring\nan end to the extreme heat plaguing the area.",
